{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And he shall not die.</b> From the inference of the negative you hear the positive: if they are upon him, he is not liable to death; but if he enters missing one of these garments, he is liable to death. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 28:35). And it is not correct in my eyes, for he should have written this verse after mentioning all eight garments (after verse 42). Why did he mention this in connection with three garments — the breastplate, the ephod, and the robe — before mentioning the forehead-plate, the tunic, the turban, the sash, and the breeches? And likewise he said, <b>and when he goes out, he shall not die</b>, but there is no service in the going out for which one would be liable on account of missing garments. And likewise, “and they shall be upon Aaron and upon his sons” (Exodus 28:43) — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 28:43): and all these garments shall be upon Aaron and upon his sons who are qualified for him and concerning whom it is written concerning them “and they shall die” — whence you learn that one who serves missing garments is liable to death. And this also appears correct according to the plain sense.\n\nBut according to what emerges from the sugyot in the Gemara, the opinion of our Sages is not so. Rather, according to them this commandment is equal for all of them, for Aaron and for his sons, and it concerns the breeches, and the punishment too is upon them, for He commanded in their making “And make for them breeches of linen” (Exodus 28:42), and He commanded concerning their donning “and they shall be upon Aaron and upon his sons” (Exodus 28:43). For concerning the rest of the garments He commanded above in their making (from verse 2 through verse 40) and commanded concerning their donning (in verse 41) “And you shall clothe Aaron your brother therewith, and his sons with him.” If so, this command (Exodus 28:43) is entirely concerning the breeches, and upon them will be the punishment. And we learned this from what they said in Sanhedrin (83a) and in Zevaḥim (17b): one who serves missing garments — from where do we know that it is by death? Rabbi Abbahu said in the name of Rabbi Yoḥanan, and they arrive at it from the teaching of Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon: “And you shall gird them with sashes, and bind turbans on them, and they shall have the priesthood as an everlasting statute” (Exodus 29:9) — when their garments are upon them, their priesthood is upon them; if their garments are not upon them, their priesthood is not upon them, and they are like laymen (zarim), and the Master said: a layman (zar) who serves — by death. And had this verse (Exodus 28:43) been, according to them, concerning all the garments, then here would be an explicit punishment for one missing garments, as the Rav said. But it concerns the breeches alone, and the other verse (Exodus 29:9) concerns the rest of the garments, and the breeches were not mentioned there. And the proof according to their opinion is that below (further, at 29:5, 9), in the order of donning, the breeches are not mentioned at all, for because He punished concerning them here, there is no need to go back and mention them, for it is known that he will don them.\n\nAnd what he said above (here), <b>and his sound shall be heard when he goes into the holy place, and he shall not die</b>, is, in my opinion, an explanation for the commandment of the bells. For since there is no need for them in the donning, and it is not the way of the dignified to make for themselves such things, therefore he said that He commanded concerning them in order that his sound be heard in the holy place, and he may enter before his Master as though with permission, for one who enters the palace of a king suddenly is liable to death by the laws of the kingdom, as in the matter of Ahasuerus (Esther 4:11). And it alludes to what they said in the Jerusalem Talmud tractate Yoma (1:5): “And no man shall be in the tent of meeting” (Leviticus 16:17) — even those of whom it is written concerning them “and the likeness of their faces was the face of a man” (Ezekiel 1:10) were not in the tent of meeting. Therefore He commanded to make his sound heard, as one who calls out “Remove every man from upon me,” and he may come to serve the King in seclusion, and likewise when he goes out — to go out with permission, and so that the matter be known, in order that the ministers of the King may return before Him. And in the chapters of the Hekhalot this matter is well known. And behold, the reason is that the angels of Elohim not strike him. And He gave this warning to the High Priest because of his exalted rank, and this is the meaning of “before HaShem,” for he is the one who serves before Him, that His Shekhinah may rest upon his service, for “the angel of HaShem of hosts is he” (Malachi 2:7), whereas the laymen (31) may enter the sanctuary to burn incense and offer.\n\nAnd I saw in the midrash Shemot Rabbah (38:8), concerning the stones of the breastplate — what is the reason? That HaShem looks upon them in the garments of the priest when he enters on the Day of Atonement and remembers the merit of the tribes. Rabbi Yehoshua in the name of Rabbi Levi says: a parable — to a king's son whose pedagogue would enter before him, pleading merit on behalf of his son, and he was afraid of those standing over him lest they strike him. What did he do? He clothed him in his own purple robe, so that they would see him and be afraid of him. So too Aaron would enter at all times (Vayikra Rabbah 22:6) into the Holy of Holies, and were it not for the many merits that would enter with him and assist him, he would not have been able to enter. Why? Because the ministering angels were there. What did HaShem do? He gave him a likeness of the garments of holiness, as it is written (Isaiah 59:17), “And He put on righteousness as a coat of armor.” Thus far this aggadah. And even though he would not enter the innermost sanctuary in these garments, even in the tent of meeting this was required on the Day of Atonement, for after all it is written (Leviticus 16:17), “And no man shall be in the tent of meeting.”"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The numeral לא (31) in the manuscript appears to reference laymen entering the sanctuary illicitly; rendered hyper-literally as 'the laymen (31) may enter the sanctuary to burn incense and offer,' preserving the obscure notation in its original position."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:18:15.215137171Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}